"Shows the distribution of geologic units and structures, distinguishing mapped rock relationships from a terrain-only view.\n\nUse when: Explain the spatial arrangement, age and character of geologic materials. Provide geological context for land and resource investigations.\n\nAvoid when: Readers require a subsurface conclusion that the available surface map cannot support.\n\nVisual grammar: Colored or patterned regions identify geologic units. Contact and fault lines use distinct confidence and type conventions. Structural symbols and a unit explanation connect observations to interpretation.\n\nBuild: Choose the map scale, geological question and authoritative source. Represent units, contacts and relevant structures using the source legend. Include unit descriptions, age relationships and uncertainty conventions.\n\nCheck: Unit identifiers correspond to the explanation. Faults and contacts retain their distinct geological meaning. Inferred, concealed and observed boundaries are not silently conflated.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a map of surface units as a complete subsurface model. Replacing the geological legend with arbitrary decorative colors.\n\nExample: A regional map distinguishes volcanic and sedimentary units, mapped faults and uncertain contacts, with a clear age and unit explanation.\n\nAlternatives: Topographic map, Reference map."
